{"reference":"2 Corinthians 4","book":"2 Corinthians","chapter":4,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter holds together fragile messengers, bodily exposure to death, inner renewal, and hope beyond what is seen as parts of one argument about ministry. Its contrasts do not divide human beings into disposable bodies and valuable inner selves.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 4:7-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_004_a815aa63ca3368ebef7e7e65"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Images","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"In verse 6, light shining in hearts is theological imagery that echoes creation and prophetic light language; it is not a scientific explanation of sight or thought. The “earthen vessels” of verses 7–12 contrast divine light and power with fragile mortal messengers, without making bodily vulnerability a moral defect. Likewise, the outer and inner person contrast addresses mortality and renewal under affliction, rather than simply setting a bad body against a good soul.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 4:16-18","2 Corinthians 4:6","2 Corinthians 4:6-12"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_004_9929cfde2da3b06d092efb05"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"The sequence of being pressed, perplexed, pursued, struck down, and yet not abandoned or destroyed keeps the jar image connected to harmed bodies and to life working in the community. The passage’s rhetoric should not turn suffering people into inspirational objects.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 4:7-15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_004_ea2f1982b8596f47e77dc507"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Why It Matters","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"The image of treasure in earthen vessels locates extraordinary power in God rather than in the messenger. It therefore resists treating apparent invulnerability, or survival of hardship, as proof of superior authority.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 4:7-15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_004_576aa20ad49ad670695e070f"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_5","text":"Paul’s testimony that affliction is not crushing is not a promise that faithful people will never experience trauma, disability, depression, breakdown, or death. His description of “light” and “momentary” affliction is a comparative claim spoken by an afflicted person and should not be used by others to minimize grief, abuse, chronic illness, or oppression.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 4:1-6","2 Corinthians 4:10-15","2 Corinthians 4:16-18","2 Corinthians 4:7-12","2 Corinthians 4:8-12"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_004_a1a90521f167d46f3ad86979"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Next Passage","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"The movement from outer decline and inner renewal continues into the following passage, where longing for a heavenly dwelling is joined with reluctance to be “unclothed,” embodied hope, and accountability. This wider context cautions against reading inner renewal as permission to dismiss pain, bodies, accessibility, treatment, or end-of-life care.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 5:1-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_004_dffffcd390e2189df8409502"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}